#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\nII. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\nIII. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1471cbdf-bc97-5a46-8981-7245c7bab5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491495} {:answer/id #uuid "48935b84-81f3-595a-95e3-e1292918d6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491590} {:answer/id #uuid "6e935aa1-98de-58bc-8f29-612955963de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491653} {:answer/id #uuid "7b157d25-3f29-59c7-994c-6b0e54448964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491670} {:answer/id #uuid "8b47ef80-7919-5404-a1ab-a8ded7329dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b47ef80-7919-5404-a1ab-a8ded7329dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491692} :answer-field/id #uuid "e746e60c-472a-557f-8568-6d42ae2542ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d18683e-9cdc-5535-b6a1-ea077ac9eff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288426" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $4x+2=4x+2$\n\nII. $-2x-1=-2x-1$\n\nIII. $3x+5=3x+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & =4x+2 \\\\ 4x+2-4x & =4x+2-4x \\\\ 2 & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x+2=4x+2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-1 & =-2x-1 \\\\ -2x-1+2x & =-2x-1+2x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x-1=-2x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & =3x+9 \\\\ 3x+5-3x & =3x+9-3x \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122da567-888b-521d-ab93-2bdadd7b97a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491491} {:answer/id #uuid "2c757179-64e0-523b-afeb-d5bc0ad04d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491543} {:answer/id #uuid "93ecc79a-c63b-50da-a503-9b6d8ce7074b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491705} {:answer/id #uuid "ab75275b-5761-5361-ae34-c628459ca7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491752} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb65399-285a-555c-b2e6-5f5e24676387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c757179-64e0-523b-afeb-d5bc0ad04d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491543} :answer-field/id #uuid "884fd6c3-8303-53d8-9178-3d82c6fc0a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc8f5f5a-7e03-528b-a06c-bf3c3858a8c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288431" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $3x+4=3x+4$\n\nII. $x-5=x-2$\n\nIII. $2x+3=x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4 & =3x+4 \\\\ 3x+4-3x & =3x+4-3x \\\\ 4 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x+4=3x+4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ -5 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =x+3 \\\\ 2x+3-x & =x+3-x \\\\ x+3 & =3 \\\\ x+3-3 & =3-3 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x+3=x+3$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26fe00c2-ff7d-5b62-96ab-0af209fd24e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295480} {:answer/id #uuid "389f9ac4-b9fe-5bf6-9689-5a77751235bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295586} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd45365-694b-59a1-9c75-444ba21c1ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295949} {:answer/id #uuid "bc73343c-08ba-5843-8a40-f11269cd8d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296258} {:answer/id #uuid "ff514a4f-a0d5-5889-8487-a38b01139445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd45365-694b-59a1-9c75-444ba21c1ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295949} :answer-field/id #uuid "632eea42-d449-505d-9c6f-a3dc34d529ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e80edf76-d1e3-576c-b5c7-753b9159f67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288440" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n-4kx=8x\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-4k,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-4k=8.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "651c6521-6052-5754-9de3-5d4d268af61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "651c6521-6052-5754-9de3-5d4d268af61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491636} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5121d0-bbcc-54ac-a60e-be4b2f5b9f0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b724bdb-17d7-5012-9f30-50cac17a9c4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-288570" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-t-2)x=x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-t-2,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-t-2=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & =3 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024ebb29-e554-55b8-8568-3de971cf842b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491452} {:answer/id #uuid "0b409fa8-7fa2-5333-b719-1bf09e74f752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491476} {:answer/id #uuid "52608292-aa25-53a5-a80a-a1687fff019e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491611} {:answer/id #uuid "e4026fca-c4a4-58f0-adbe-16c380971427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491844} {:answer/id #uuid "f0569689-8bdd-5f6e-84ed-219724f09b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4026fca-c4a4-58f0-adbe-16c380971427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9275af45-98fb-5d13-b62c-71ff39cdad22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fff83f5-94fb-54ad-8709-ecc3c59291a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288574" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+2)x+r=5x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+2)x+r=5x+3\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+2=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nr=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=3$ and $r=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=3+3=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b78bfb02-4c20-5a75-811c-305086ea1983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73256cc5-b18c-5ffc-bb1b-9018e84f6eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288583" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$"}]}
